-- v1 -> v2: add the per-asset capture date, so imported photos sort by when they were taken
-- (set on PHAssetCreationRequest.creationDate) rather than the download/import time.
--
-- NON-destructive ADD COLUMN with a default — unlike the ledger's destructive migrations, the
-- downloadAsset table holds PERMANENT suppression rows (the `createdLocalId` set that stops the upload
-- extension from re-uploading downloaded photos). Dropping it would lose echo-suppression, so we add
-- the column in place. Pre-migration rows get an empty creationDate (harmless: they are already
-- IMPORTED and are never re-imported). Migration SQL is raw (no `AS DownloadState` annotation).
--
-- ⚠️ KNOWN, DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE FROM `DownloadStore.sq` — decided, not overlooked. This statement gives
-- `creationDate` a `DEFAULT ''`; the CREATE statement declares it `TEXT NOT NULL` with no default. The
-- migrated column also lands LAST, where the CREATE statement puts it before `createdLocalId`. So a
-- migrated `downloadAsset` and a created one differ in two ways.
--
-- NEITHER IS REACHABLE. Every statement in `DownloadStore.sq` names its columns, so ordinal position never
-- leaks; the column is NOT NULL and every insert supplies it, so the default is never applied.
--
-- IT IS NOT FIXED because fixing it means REBUILDING the table — SQLite can neither reorder a column nor
-- alter a default in place — which puts real suppression rows through a copy to erase a difference nothing
-- can observe. The committed schema snapshot (`databases/3.db`) is generated from the CREATE statements,
-- so it neither detects nor repairs this; it verifies migrations from v3 forward. Decision record:
-- `changes/archive/2026-09-20-repair-unrun-checks` (D4). If you are replaying the chain by hand and
-- found this: you have found the known one, and the ledger's chain is clean.
ALTER TABLE downloadAsset ADD COLUMN creationDate TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '';
